1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,752
This video covers three short topics from the course notes. How hard is a problem? What kind of machine solves it? And what styles of programming language are there?

2
00:00:11,202 --> 00:00:21,314
At the end we solve all the short answer questions in questions-pre 12.1 and 12.2. Many of these come from past midterms.

3
00:00:21,764 --> 00:00:25,817
Engineers ask three practical questions about a problem.

4
00:00:26,267 --> 00:00:33,307
First: how many steps does the computer need? The notes sort problems into easy, hard and impossible.

5
00:00:33,757 --> 00:00:42,141
Second: what kind of machine can solve it? We meet four machines: F S M, P D A, L B A and Turing machine.

6
00:00:42,591 --> 00:00:49,482
Third: what style of language do we write the solution in? These styles are called programming paradigms.

7
00:00:49,932 --> 00:01:01,516
Big-O notation gives the asymptotic complexity of an algorithm. Usually it counts time steps, and sometimes memory. The vertical axis shows the steps.

8
00:01:01,966 --> 00:01:06,574
Let n be the size of the input. It is on the horizontal axis.

9
00:01:07,024 --> 00:01:09,861
A linear algorithm takes about n steps.

10
00:01:10,311 --> 00:01:14,514
n log n grows a little faster. Quick sort is an example.

11
00:01:14,964 --> 00:01:19,081
n squared grows faster again. Bubble sort is an example.

12
00:01:19,531 --> 00:01:29,345
2 to the n is exponential. It leaves the chart before n reaches 9: 2 to the 9 is 512, more than 400.

13
00:01:29,795 --> 00:01:34,595
Now take n equals 20. A linear algorithm takes 20 steps.

14
00:01:35,045 --> 00:01:43,791
Log 20 is about 4.32. So n log n is about 20 times 4.32, or 86 steps.

15
00:01:44,241 --> 00:01:48,231
n squared is 20 times 20, or 400 steps.

16
00:01:48,681 --> 00:01:56,510
2 to the 20 is 1,048,576 steps. That is more than one million.

17
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:09,568
Each extra input item doubles the work. At n equals 21, 2 to the n is 2 times 2 to the 20: 2,097,152 steps.

18
00:02:10,018 --> 00:02:20,365
This table from the notes lists the common classes. The first six rows are polynomial: constant, log, linear, n log n, quadratic and cubic.

19
00:02:20,815 --> 00:02:23,119
Together they form the class P TIME.

20
00:02:23,569 --> 00:02:27,323
Below them are the N P complete problems, such as sat.

21
00:02:27,773 --> 00:02:32,744
The naive way to solve sat takes 2 to the n steps. That is exponential.

22
00:02:33,194 --> 00:02:36,117
The bounded halting problem is in EXPTIME.

23
00:02:36,567 --> 00:02:45,655
At the bottom are the undecidable problems. The halting problem is the famous example. No algorithm solves it, at any speed.

24
00:02:46,105 --> 00:02:56,515
In ECE 351 the important line is between polynomial and exponential. All polynomial problems count as easy.

25
00:02:56,965 --> 00:03:05,541
In big-O notation, only the dominant term matters. Take f of n equals 7 n squared plus n plus 100.

26
00:03:05,991 --> 00:03:15,186
At n equals 10, 7 n squared is 700. n plus 100 is 110. The total is 810.

27
00:03:15,636 --> 00:03:23,615
So the 7 n squared term is 700 out of 810. That is already 86 percent of the total.

28
00:03:24,065 --> 00:03:33,537
At n equals 100, 7 n squared is 70,000 out of 70,200. That is 99.7 percent.

29
00:03:33,987 --> 00:03:47,043
At n equals 1000, 7 n squared is 7 million out of 7,001,100. That is 99.98 percent. The other terms are almost nothing.

30
00:03:47,493 --> 00:03:50,757
So we drop the small terms, n and 100.

31
00:03:51,207 --> 00:03:55,964
And we drop the constant factor 7. The answer is O of n squared.

32
00:03:56,414 --> 00:04:01,833
The notes give the same rule: O of n squared plus n is written O of n squared.

33
00:04:02,283 --> 00:04:12,693
The standard N P complete problem is boolean satisfiability, or sat. You get a boolean formula. Here the formula is x and y and z.

34
00:04:13,143 --> 00:04:19,053
The variables are true or false, written 1 and 0. The operators are and, or, and not.

35
00:04:19,503 --> 00:04:29,764
Find values for the variables that make the formula true. The brute force method lists the truth table: every row of values for x, y and z.

36
00:04:30,214 --> 00:04:34,481
It checks the rows one by one. Rows 1 to 4 give 0.

37
00:04:34,931 --> 00:04:37,469
Rows 5 to 7 also give 0.

38
00:04:37,919 --> 00:04:42,229
The last row, 1, 1, 1, gives 1. This is the solution.

39
00:04:42,679 --> 00:04:46,135
Three variables give 2 to the 3, or 8 rows.

40
00:04:46,585 --> 00:04:54,265
Each new variable doubles the table. 10 variables give 2 to the 10, or 1024 rows.

41
00:04:54,715 --> 00:05:01,691
20 variables give 1,048,576 rows, about one million.

42
00:05:02,141 --> 00:05:08,477
30 variables give more than one billion rows. So brute force takes exponential time.

43
00:05:08,927 --> 00:05:21,364
N P stands for non-deterministic polynomial time. The notes give four views of it. View a: if someone gives us a solution, we can verify it in polynomial time.

44
00:05:21,814 --> 00:05:27,211
For the guess 1, 1, 1, we substitute the values: 1 and 1 and 1 is 1.

45
00:05:27,661 --> 00:05:32,312
A defining feature of N P complete problems is this fast check.

46
00:05:32,762 --> 00:05:40,143
The tree shows every choice for x, y and z. Each of the 8 leaves is one row of the truth table.

47
00:05:40,593 --> 00:05:52,177
View b: a magic, non-deterministic machine always guesses the right next step. It picks x equals 1, then y equals 1, then z equals 1.

48
00:05:52,627 --> 00:05:59,390
It finds a solution in a polynomial number of guesses. Here: 3 guesses for 3 variables.

49
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:06,091
View c: a computer with an exponential number of cores. Each core tries a different path.

50
00:06:06,541 --> 00:06:11,810
With 2 to the 3, or 8 cores, all 8 rows are checked at the same time.

51
00:06:12,260 --> 00:06:19,023
View d: a normal single-core deterministic computer. It checks the rows one after the other.

52
00:06:19,473 --> 00:06:24,763
In the worst case it checks all 2 to the n rows. That is exponential time.

53
00:06:25,213 --> 00:06:28,221
Sat is the canonical N P complete problem.

54
00:06:28,671 --> 00:06:35,754
Other common examples are travelling salesman, scheduling, knapsack or bin packing, and graph colouring.

55
00:06:36,204 --> 00:06:44,055
All of them can be used to encode sat. Such a reduction is the standard way to prove that a problem is N P complete.

56
00:06:44,505 --> 00:06:55,939
When you meet a new problem, ask if it is similar to a known N P complete problem. For example, register allocation in a compiler is similar to graph colouring.

57
00:06:56,389 --> 00:07:03,195
The course uses two of them: register allocation, by graph colouring, and circuit equivalence, by sat.

58
00:07:03,645 --> 00:07:10,215
Here is our own small example of the link. Each circle is a variable: a, b, c and d.

59
00:07:10,665 --> 00:07:16,852
An edge joins two variables that are live at the same time. They cannot share a register.

60
00:07:17,302 --> 00:07:22,337
With 3 registers, give each variable a colour, one colour per register.

61
00:07:22,787 --> 00:07:26,712
No edge joins two circles of the same colour, so this works.

62
00:07:27,162 --> 00:07:31,727
a and d have no edge between them. So they can share register 1.

63
00:07:32,177 --> 00:07:38,151
With 2 registers, a, b and c form a triangle. They need three different colours.

64
00:07:38,601 --> 00:07:49,886
A brute force search tries all 2 to the 4, or 16, colourings. In each one, two of a, b and c get the same colour, as the red lines show. So none works.

65
00:07:50,336 --> 00:07:54,944
So one variable, here c, must go to memory. This is called a spill.

66
00:07:55,394 --> 00:08:02,455
Compilers contain several hard, N P complete problems. The notes give three ways to handle one.

67
00:08:02,905 --> 00:08:10,201
Way a: use a polynomial time approximation algorithm. This is the most common way in practice.

68
00:08:10,651 --> 00:08:21,190
Way b: implement an N P complete algorithm yourself. The notes say this is never done in compilers, and almost never done anywhere else.

69
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:31,389
Way c: translate the problem into a boolean formula and ask a sat solver for the answer. The notes recommend this way for your career.

70
00:08:31,839 --> 00:08:40,778
On the exam, you solve small N P complete problems on paper. The course does not study approximation algorithms.

71
00:08:41,228 --> 00:08:50,039
Some problems cannot be solved on a computer at all. They are called undecidable. The standard example is the halting problem.

72
00:08:50,489 --> 00:08:58,019
Given a program P and an input i, prove that P halts on i. That is, P does not get stuck in a loop.

73
00:08:58,469 --> 00:09:06,747
For many programs this is easy. A program with no loops and no recursion, like hello world, halts on every input.

74
00:09:07,197 --> 00:09:16,135
With a loop, the answer can depend on the input. This loop is our own example: while x is not 1, x becomes x minus 2.

75
00:09:16,585 --> 00:09:23,092
With x equal to 5, x goes 5, 3, 1. Then the loop stops, so the program halts.

76
00:09:23,542 --> 00:09:32,033
With x equal to 4, x goes 4, 2, 0, minus 2, and so on. x never equals 1, so it runs forever.

77
00:09:32,483 --> 00:09:40,525
In the 1930s, Alan Turing found a program and an input for which a proof of halting is impossible.

78
00:09:40,975 --> 00:09:49,018
One such case is enough. So the problem is undecidable in general. No program answers it for all P and i.

79
00:09:49,468 --> 00:10:00,412
A corollary of the halting problem: for two programs P and Q in a Turing complete language, we cannot decide, in general, if P and Q are equivalent.

80
00:10:00,862 --> 00:10:09,374
This matters for compilers. You add a new optimization. How do you know the optimized program is equivalent to the original?

81
00:10:09,824 --> 00:10:17,632
In practice, compiler engineers run the compiled programs P prime and Q prime on the same input i.

82
00:10:18,082 --> 00:10:21,218
They check that the outputs p and q are the same.

83
00:10:21,668 --> 00:10:28,793
This is weaker than a proof. There might be an input x that we did not test, on which the outputs differ.

84
00:10:29,243 --> 00:10:33,915
Now the course definitions. Polynomial time algorithms are easy.

85
00:10:34,365 --> 00:10:40,189
Exponential time algorithms are hard. N P complete problems are in this group.

86
00:10:40,639 --> 00:10:43,349
Undecidable problems are impossible.

87
00:10:43,799 --> 00:10:48,257
This classification is about how many steps the computer must run.

88
00:10:48,707 --> 00:10:58,179
It is not about how much work the engineer does to write the code. Quick sort is tricky to implement, but it runs quickly. So here it is easy.

89
00:10:58,629 --> 00:11:06,309
The notes describe four theoretical machines. A finite state machine has no storage beyond its current state.

90
00:11:06,759 --> 00:11:15,805
It consumes one input token per step, so it always runs in linear time. F S Ms recognize regular languages.

91
00:11:16,255 --> 00:11:20,948
A push-down automaton is an F S M plus an infinite stack.

92
00:11:21,398 --> 00:11:27,819
P D As recognize context-free languages, and they always run in polynomial time.

93
00:11:28,269 --> 00:11:33,048
A linear-bounded automaton is an F S M plus a finite tape.

94
00:11:33,498 --> 00:11:38,085
The physical computers we use every day are, strictly, L B As.

95
00:11:38,535 --> 00:11:42,631
A Turing machine is an F S M plus an infinite tape.

96
00:11:43,081 --> 00:11:52,297
We usually think of our computers as Turing machines, but technically they are L B As. The Turing machine is a model for theory.

97
00:11:52,747 --> 00:11:58,699
In all four, the controller has a finite number of states. The power grows with the storage.

98
00:11:59,149 --> 00:12:12,653
Does non-determinism make a machine more powerful? It depends on the machine. For finite state machines, no. D F As and N F As have the same power, and both run in linear time.

99
00:12:13,103 --> 00:12:17,881
But an N F A might have exponentially fewer states than the D F A.

100
00:12:18,331 --> 00:12:27,867
For push-down automata, yes. Some context-free grammars need a non-deterministic P D A. The course does not use this fact.

101
00:12:28,317 --> 00:12:36,936
For Turing machines, non-determinism gives speed, but not power. The set of problems that can be computed is the same.

102
00:12:37,386 --> 00:12:45,215
But the non-deterministic machine computes some problems exponentially faster. These are the N P complete problems.

103
00:12:45,665 --> 00:12:54,071
One basic difference between a Turing machine and a real computer is the storage. A Turing machine has an infinite tape.

104
00:12:54,521 --> 00:12:57,059
A real computer has finite memory.

105
00:12:57,509 --> 00:13:02,309
Inside the processor there is only a small, fixed number of registers.

106
00:13:02,759 --> 00:13:13,255
This shows up in compilers as the register allocation problem. Here three variables, a, b and c, need registers, but there are only two.

107
00:13:13,705 --> 00:13:20,703
If the variables do not fit in the registers, some variables spill into main memory, which is much slower.

108
00:13:21,153 --> 00:13:28,299
A programming language is Turing complete if it can describe any computation that a Turing machine can do.

109
00:13:28,749 --> 00:13:35,747
Roughly, it needs conditionals, loops or recursion, and a potentially unlimited number of variables.

110
00:13:36,197 --> 00:13:41,338
Almost every programming language is Turing complete. Java and C are.

111
00:13:41,788 --> 00:13:46,076
TeX, the document language under LaTeX, is Turing complete.

112
00:13:46,526 --> 00:13:50,003
So is PostScript, a page language for printers.

113
00:13:50,453 --> 00:13:53,269
HTML is not Turing complete.

114
00:13:53,719 --> 00:14:07,522
SQL is not Turing complete either. For that reason some people do not call HTML and SQL programming languages. The notes do not list the features of SQL, so those cells are empty.

115
00:14:07,972 --> 00:14:10,937
PDF is also not Turing complete.

116
00:14:11,387 --> 00:14:19,025
A Turing complete language can loop forever. So a PostScript file can make a printer get stuck in an infinite loop.

117
00:14:19,475 --> 00:14:29,459
Programming languages come in styles, called paradigms. Imperative languages have two features: re-assignment and sequential composition.

118
00:14:29,909 --> 00:14:35,669
Re-assignment: the value of a variable can change. Here x gets the value 7.

119
00:14:36,119 --> 00:14:42,177
Sequential composition: statements execute one after the other. This line prints 7.

120
00:14:42,627 --> 00:14:46,553
Now x is re-assigned. Its value changes to 2.

121
00:14:47,003 --> 00:14:51,611
The second print gives 2. The same name has two values at two times.

122
00:14:52,061 --> 00:15:02,109
Re-assignment is convenient, but it makes programs harder to reason about. The keyword final prevents it, so the program is easier to reason about.

123
00:15:02,559 --> 00:15:07,295
The theoretical model for imperative languages is the Turing machine.

124
00:15:07,745 --> 00:15:14,977
Functional languages have three features. First, immutable data: variables cannot be re-assigned.

125
00:15:15,427 --> 00:15:23,597
Second, anonymous functions, written with lambda. This one takes x and y, and returns true if x is less than y.

126
00:15:24,047 --> 00:15:29,829
Third, higher-order functions. A higher-order function takes a function as an argument.

127
00:15:30,279 --> 00:15:33,372
The sort function takes a list and a comparator.

128
00:15:33,822 --> 00:15:40,713
Give sort the comparator x less than y. The result is in ascending order: 1, 2, 5, 8.

129
00:15:41,163 --> 00:15:48,587
Give the same sort the comparator x greater than y. Now the order is descending: 8, 5, 2, 1.

130
00:15:49,037 --> 00:15:53,495
The input list does not change. It is still 5, 2, 8, 1.

131
00:15:53,945 --> 00:16:01,945
The theoretical model for functional languages is the lambda calculus. It has the same power as Turing machines.

132
00:16:02,395 --> 00:16:10,374
This table from the notes sums up the paradigms. Imperative languages have assignment and sequential composition.

133
00:16:10,824 --> 00:16:18,205
Object-oriented languages have objects. They might have classes, inheritance and dynamic dispatch.

134
00:16:18,655 --> 00:16:25,375
Functional languages have higher-order functions and lambdas, and data are immutable by default.

135
00:16:25,825 --> 00:16:33,143
Logic languages, such as Prolog and SQL, have logical equations and an engine that evaluates them.

136
00:16:33,593 --> 00:16:41,763
The 01a slides call functional and logic languages declarative: you say what to compute, not how.

137
00:16:42,213 --> 00:16:56,635
Many modern languages mix these styles. In the labs we use Java, an imperative object-oriented language, in a functional style. We limit re-assignment with final and use some higher-order functions.

138
00:16:57,085 --> 00:17:05,149
Now the short answer questions in questions-pre 12.1. Here are the first five. Pause the video and try them. 

139
00:17:05,599 --> 00:17:22,431
12.1.1. A Turing machine has an infinite tape, and a real computer has finite memory. In a compiler this is the register allocation problem. Variables that do not fit in the registers spill into slower main memory.

140
00:17:22,881 --> 00:17:35,766
12.1.2. Any four of: sat, travelling salesman, knapsack, graph colouring, register allocation, Hamiltonian path, clique, vertex cover, or subgraph isomorphism.

141
00:17:36,216 --> 00:17:39,949
12.1.3. Linear time is O of n.

142
00:17:40,399 --> 00:17:44,069
12.1.4. O of 1 is constant time.

143
00:17:44,519 --> 00:17:52,476
12.1.5. The halting problem. It shows the limits of what compilers can understand about programs.

144
00:17:52,926 --> 00:17:56,787
Here are the next five questions. Pause the video and try them. 

145
00:17:57,237 --> 00:18:12,341
12.1.6. Use a polynomial approximation. Translate the problem for a sat solver. Or write a custom solution by hand. The notes add that the custom solution is never done in compilers.

146
00:18:12,791 --> 00:18:19,341
12.1.7. Yes. Non-deterministic push-down automata are more powerful.

147
00:18:19,791 --> 00:18:26,532
12.1.8. No. Non-determinism gives a Turing machine speed, but not power.

148
00:18:26,982 --> 00:18:36,411
12.1.9. Keep the dominant term, 7 n squared, and drop the constant 7. The answer is O of n squared.

149
00:18:36,861 --> 00:18:42,621
12.1.11. Conditionals, and repetition by loops or recursion.

150
00:18:43,071 --> 00:18:46,506
The next three questions. Pause the video and try them. 

151
00:18:46,956 --> 00:19:04,471
12.1.10. questions-pre has no posted solution, so this is our solution. Draw two overlapping circles, A and B. Shade the part of A that is outside B. That region is A minus B: the elements in A that are not in B.

152
00:19:04,921 --> 00:19:09,251
12.1.12. HTML or PDF.

153
00:19:09,701 --> 00:19:19,109
12.1.13. The printer can get stuck in an infinite loop. This cannot happen with a language that is not Turing complete.

154
00:19:19,559 --> 00:19:23,037
The next six questions. Pause the video and try them. 

155
00:19:23,487 --> 00:19:37,460
12.1.14. The halting problem is to prove that a program halts. The posted solution says: on all possible inputs. The notes say: for a program P and one input i.

156
00:19:37,910 --> 00:19:48,598
12.1.15. Alan Turing. Gödel invented the technique that Turing used, but Gödel's proof was about logic, not computation.

157
00:19:49,048 --> 00:19:57,944
12.1.16. Easy means polynomial time complexity, for example O of n or O of n squared.

158
00:19:58,394 --> 00:20:07,311
12.1.17. Hard means N P complete: exponential time complexity, such as O of 2 to the n.

159
00:20:07,761 --> 00:20:14,865
12.1.18. Impossible means undecidable: a Turing machine cannot compute it.

160
00:20:15,315 --> 00:20:26,302
12.1.19. No. These terms are not about the effort of the programmer. They measure the number of steps the machine must take to solve the problem.

161
00:20:26,752 --> 00:20:31,552
The last six questions of 12.1. Pause the video and try them. 

162
00:20:32,002 --> 00:20:37,613
12.1.20. N P stands for non-deterministic polynomial.

163
00:20:38,063 --> 00:20:42,884
12.1.21. Exponential time, O of 2 to the n.

164
00:20:43,334 --> 00:20:50,267
12.1.22. Use a known polynomial heuristic, that is, an approximation.

165
00:20:50,717 --> 00:20:57,608
12.1.23. Translate the problem to a boolean formula and use a sat solver.

166
00:20:58,058 --> 00:21:06,975
12.1.24. Write an exhaustive search algorithm by hand. This works, but it is never done in compilers.

167
00:21:07,425 --> 00:21:20,503
12.1.25. Given a boolean formula, find values for the variables that make the formula true. That is, find a row in the truth table that evaluates to true.

168
00:21:20,953 --> 00:21:26,350
Now questions-pre 12.2, about automata. Pause the video and try them.

169
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:37,125
12.2.1. First, a D F A cannot have edges labelled epsilon. An N F A can, like the edge from 0 to 1 here.

170
00:21:37,575 --> 00:21:49,607
Second, in a D F A all edges that leave a state have different labels. An N F A can have several edges with the same label, like the two a edges that leave state 1.

171
00:21:50,057 --> 00:21:52,553
Both machines accept a, and a b.

172
00:21:53,003 --> 00:21:59,275
12.2.2. A finite automaton, also called a finite state machine.

173
00:21:59,725 --> 00:22:06,296
12.2.3. Linear time, O of n. The machine takes one step per input letter.

174
00:22:06,746 --> 00:22:10,245
The last three questions. Pause the video and try them.

175
00:22:10,695 --> 00:22:22,193
12.2.4. No. Every N F A can be converted to a D F A, by the subset construction. And every D F A already is an N F A.

176
00:22:22,643 --> 00:22:30,387
12.2.5. No. Notes section 3.11 shows that the two notations convert to each other.

177
00:22:30,837 --> 00:22:37,621
An ECE 327 machine has internal variables, like x on these edges.

178
00:22:38,071 --> 00:22:48,226
The ECE 351 machine puts them into the state labels. So S1 becomes S1 x 0 and S1 x 1.

179
00:22:48,676 --> 00:22:59,684
12.2.6. Yes. The ECE 327 notation is higher level. It is convenient for people, but it adds no power.

180
00:23:00,134 --> 00:23:09,905
To summarize. Big-O keeps only the dominant term. Polynomial is easy, exponential is hard, and undecidable is impossible.

181
00:23:10,355 --> 00:23:23,475
For an N P problem, a solution can be checked in polynomial time. Sat is the canonical N P complete problem. Handle a hard problem with an approximation or a sat solver.

182
00:23:23,925 --> 00:23:33,333
Turing showed that the halting problem is undecidable. So program equivalence is undecidable, and compilers test outputs instead.

183
00:23:33,783 --> 00:23:45,132
The four machines gain power with storage. Non-determinism adds power to the push-down automaton, but not to the finite state machine or the Turing machine.

